{% block titre %}Alleluia. Dies sanctificatus{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}The Alleluia Dies sanctificatus, Mass of Christmas Day: the form of the Alleluia and its jubilus, that great melisma of joy on the final « a ».{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>Example 5 — <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia. Dies sanctificatus</span></h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    The acclamation that precedes the Gospel on Christmas Day. A chant built on a single
    word — <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span> — and on the long vocalise that prolongs
    it&nbsp;: the <strong>jubilus</strong>.
</p>

<div class="fiche">
    <dl>
        <dt>Genre</dt><dd>Alleluia (acclamation before the Gospel)</dd>
        <dt>Occasion</dt><dd>Mass of Christmas Day (<span class="mot-latin">in die</span>), 25 December</dd>
        <dt>Text</dt><dd>Proper verse for Christmas&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Dies sanctificatus illuxit nobis…</span></dd>
        <dt>Mode</dt><dd>Mode II (low <em>re</em> mode, recollected)</dd>
        <dt>Style</dt><dd><strong>Melismatic</strong>&nbsp;: the word is short, the vocalise long</dd>
        <dt>In the <em>Triplex</em></dt><dd>p. 49</dd>
    </dl>
</div>

<div class="lecteur-audio">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen to the chant</span>
    <p class="note-audio">Watch for the return of the <span class="mot-latin">jubilus</span>&nbsp;: it opens the chant, then comes back after the verse.</p>
